# House lint configuration for hvtiRutilities.
#
# ⚠️ This file is DCF, not R: `read.dcf()` parses it, so a value continues
# only while its lines are indented and an INDENTED `#` terminates the value
# and starts a bogus record. Every comment therefore has to sit at column 0,
# above the key it explains, which is why the reasoning below is not beside
# the linters it describes.
#
# This file exists because the `lint` job did not have one. Without a
# `.lintr` lintr fell back to its defaults -- 80-character lines among them --
# and reported 290 lints on `main`. The job passed anyway: `lint_package()`
# *prints* its result rather than failing on it, so the green check meant only
# that R had started. `lint.yaml` now sets `LINTR_ERROR_ON_LINT`, which makes
# lintr's print method stop() on a non-empty result. That turns the numbers
# below into a gate, so every entry here has to be a decision rather than a
# convenience.
#
# line_length 135 rather than lintr's 80, for the same reason and with the
# same number as hvtiRtemplates. `hvti_taxonomy()` is a data table written as
# code -- column-aligned rows whose alignment is the only thing that makes the
# prefix system readable down the page -- and it accounts for 45 of the 196
# line-length lints on its own. Wrapping it to 80 would destroy that
# alignment. The widest line in the package is 132 characters, which is also
# hvtiRtemplates' measurement of the same table; 135 leaves a little room
# without inviting drift.
#
# `commented_code_linter` is OFF: it fires on comments that are prose, not
# code. lintr parses `# WBC (K/uL)` as a call to `WBC()` and
# `# alpha/analyses/qmd/03.01-ac.qmd` as division, so a unit comment and a
# file path both read as commented-out code. Worse, the arithmetic derivations
# in `test-proc_means_weighted_values.R` -- `# sum(w*v) = 1 + 2 + 6 + 16 = 25`
# -- are the record of where each expected value comes from, which is the most
# valuable comment in a numeric test and also the most parseable. Of the 15 it
# reported, none was commented-out code.
#
# `object_name_linter` is OFF: the package's constant convention is
# SCREAMING_CASE and lintr's default style is snake_case. Widening to
# `SNAKE_CASE` covers the exported `POOL_AFFIXES` / `POOL_PLAIN_SUFFIX` /
# `POOL_MIN_STEM` and the internal `.VARIANT_SUFFIXES` / `.NON_SOURCE_RE` /
# `.STATS`, but it silently drops the `symbols` style that the default list
# carries, which is what lets `%||%` pass -- so widening the list breaks an
# infix definition. Three further cases survive any style list and cannot be
# renamed: `.Random.seed` and `.onAttach` are R's own names, and
# `hvtiRutilities.news()` is EXPORTED, so renaming it to satisfy a linter
# would break callers to tidy up a warning. Enforcing the rule would mean a
# path-keyed exclusion list for the irreducible files, and path keys go stale
# silently on a rename -- renaming nine files in hvtiRtemplates took its lint
# count from 0 to 86 in one step. One documented `NULL` beats a list that rots.
#
# `brace_linter` is ON but with `allow_single_line = TRUE`, which is the only
# configuration that admits a standalone nested block. `test-cache_key.R` tests
# that `{ a <- 1 }` nested inside another block binds `a` for what follows it,
# so the block IS the subject of the test. The linter's default insists an
# opening brace never starts a line -- it should trail `function(x)`, `if (...)`
# and so on -- which a standalone block cannot do, and splitting it across
# lines still leaves one lint. Measured: single-line 2 lints, multi-line 1,
# and 0 only with this option. Every other brace rule stays in force.
#
# Every OTHER default linter is ON and enforced, indentation, commas, infix
# spaces and object usage included. Those reported 69 lints once the width and
# the two exemptions above were set. 67 were FIXED in the source. The other two
# are the `test-cache_key.R` nested block described above, which no source
# change can satisfy, so `allow_single_line` covers them and the test is
# untouched. There are no path exclusions at all. Keep it that way: an
# exclusion here is invisible in a passing run, which is the same failure shape
# as the job this file was written to repair.
linters: linters_with_defaults(
    line_length_linter = line_length_linter(135L),
    brace_linter = brace_linter(allow_single_line = TRUE),
    commented_code_linter = NULL,
    object_name_linter = NULL
  )
